From 288b1c21d108580df8da6f2494945d4c4cefee80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex Executor <codex-executor@local>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:21:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/24] sheet route: a cut through a cell edge is a status, not
 a crash

The census of record lost one cell without a JSON line: the 45 degree
centred anchor, whose plane x + y = 1 CONTAINS two vertical cell edges.
The exact chart compiler refuses such a plane ("patch box_x_min has
coincident semantic edges"), and the refusal happens in geometry
compilation, before the mesher's own error handling, so both the census
tool and the production command died with a traceback and produced no
record at all.  Measured across a sweep of vertical planes: the compiler
refuses exactly when the plane contains a cell edge, that is when the
local offset d equals one of 0, a, b, a + b, and accepts every other
offset.

sheet_label_pipeline.cut_contains_a_cell_edge decides this by exact
rational arithmetic on the cell's local cut plane, before any meshing.
The production command now writes a receipt with status
GEOMETRY_DEGENERATE, the census tool prints the same status on its JSON
line, and the population generator moves its 45 degree anchor off the
degenerate offset (0.53 instead of 0.50).

The remedy for a real structure is in the receipt: move the WORLD cut
plane by one exact rational epsilon.  All cells keep the same plane, so
the shared-face carrier traces still agree exactly (verified today for a
cut crossing a shared face: identical node and triangle sets on both
sides).
